{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Prototype.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Prototype.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"prototype prototype","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Prototype.md","fileName":"Prototype.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Prototype","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 13:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Software Engineering]] [[Game Design Methods]] [[Game Production Planning and Milestones]] [[Iterative Game Design Cycle]] [[Game Prototyping Strategy]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Software Engineering","Game Design Methods","Game Production Planning and Milestones","Iterative Game Design Cycle","Game Prototyping Strategy","3D User Interface","Formative Evaluation","Design-Build-Test Loop","Spike","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","advancedgamedesign.pdf","agilegamedevelopment2e.pdf","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Prototype.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Prototype.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"prototype prototype","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Prototype.md","fileName":"Prototype.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Prototype","heading":"Prototype","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A prototype is a representation of a design used to explore, communicate, or evaluate it before the final system is complete. Its fidelity describes how closely and completely it represents the intended result.\n\nEarly prototypes can be inexpensive and incomplete when the question concerns layout or flow. A [[3D User Interface]] often requires a more functional prototype because spatial mappings, devices, movement, and sensory feedback are difficult to judge on paper.\n\nPrototypes make imagined details concrete. They support rapid [[Formative Evaluation]], reveal flawed assumptions, and allow repeated design changes before production work is fixed.\n\nFor a game, a prototype must include at least one working interactive loop; a static mockup, prerecorded demonstration, or noninteractive simulation does not test gameplay. Each prototype should answer a clear design question, remain separate from production code, and be cheap enough to discard after it informs the next [[Design-Build-Test Loop|iteration]].\n\nIn an agile backlog, a timeboxed [[Spike]] can use a prototype to remove uncertainty before estimation or commitment. Prototyping is especially important for novel platforms, where changing hardware constraints make long-range designs assumption-heavy and several parallel options may need to be tested.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Software Engineering","Game Design Methods","Game Production Planning and Milestones","Iterative Game Design Cycle","Game Prototyping Strategy","3D User Interface","Formative Evaluation","Design-Build-Test Loop","Spike","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","advancedgamedesign.pdf","agilegamedevelopment2e.pdf","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Prototype.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Prototype.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"prototype prototype","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Prototype.md","fileName":"Prototype.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Prototype","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf]]\n[[advancedgamedesign.pdf]]\n[[agilegamedevelopment2e.pdf]]\n[[gamesdesignandplay.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Software Engineering","Game Design Methods","Game Production Planning and Milestones","Iterative Game Design Cycle","Game Prototyping Strategy","3D User Interface","Formative Evaluation","Design-Build-Test Loop","Spike","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","advancedgamedesign.pdf","agilegamedevelopment2e.pdf","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}